+# ADR-0017: ERP port operation to read advisor master data
+
+- **Status:** Proposed
+- **Date:** 2026-11-04
+- **Deciders:** <names>
+- **Sources:** T2-C-010, T3-D-005, T3-AC-001, T2-D-009, AN-902
+
+## Context
+
+Head office needs the advisor master data that the ERP runs: advisor number (`B` + 5 digits), given name, family name, e-mail and an active flag (T2-C-010). The ERP port has one operation, `readCustomer`. T3-D-005 and T3-AC-001 allow ERP access only through the `integration` module and its port, so a new read needs a port operation and a payload contract. Every read records its source version and read time (T2-D-009). Architecture section 4 puts advisor master data under the reads of `identity` through `integration`.
+
+## Options
+
+1. **A new port operation `readAdvisor` with its own contract `AdvisorMasterData`, read live.** Good: same shape as `readCustomer`; small; the NAV adapter maps one more NAV call. Bad: a synchronous ERP read outside the reads that T2 section 4 names (see spec AN-902, open question 2).
+2. **Extend `CustomerMasterData` with advisor fields.** Good: no new operation. Bad: an advisor is not a customer record in the ERP; the keys differ (`K` + 6 digits against `B` + 5 digits); it breaks the customer contract.
+3. **Read from an `erp_copy` table kept by the inbound flow T2-C-010.** Good: no synchronous ERP call; works during an outage. Bad: needs a table, a sync job and a maximum age that no requirement gives yet; larger than the story.
+
+## Decision
+
+We choose option 1, unless the approver answers open question 2 of AN-902 with a copy. It adds `ErpPort.readAdvisor(advisorNumber): Promise<AdvisorMasterData | null>` with the Zod schema `AdvisorMasterData { advisorNumber, givenName, familyName, email, active, sourceVersion }`, and `Integration.readAdvisor` returning `ErpRead<AdvisorMasterData> | null`. `identity` receives `Integration` as a dependency and exposes `getAdvisor`. It keeps the port contract-first and the ERP access in one module, and option 3 can replace the source later behind the same `identity` API.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- `identity` now depends on the public API of `integration` (the direction that architecture section 4 already gives). `createIdentity` needs an `integration` dependency.
+- Each ERP adapter (`erp-fake` now, `erp-nav` and `erp-bc` later) must implement `readAdvisor`; the type check fails otherwise.
+- During an ERP outage `getAdvisor` throws `ErpUnavailableError`; the caller decides the fallback.
+- `pnpm arch` still enforces that only `integration` imports `@provital/erp-port`.
